A||||4||daved||axle reinstall, seems too tight||||1||
Z||000000||daved||05-12-2001||06:32 PM||||Got any suggestions on how to re-install the passenger side axle?  This is on an '86 GT, though I think they are all the same.<BR>I removed it fairly easily, and installed Rodney's seal/bearing assembly.  Now the axle refuses to snap home. I'm afraid to pound on it...various people have warned against driving it in if you are not sure it has engaged the splines.  I do think it is in the splines as far as the snap ring, as I can hear the diff. turning when I rotate the axle.  Any advice?  Thanks,  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||daved||05-13-2001||12:26 AM||||Come on Guys;  There's got to be a trick to this.  Cars in the air till I can get the blasted axle back in!  Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||05-13-2001||12:27 AM||||1 2 3 PUUUUSSHHH!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||05-13-2001||06:26 AM||||There should be a lip between the boot and the splines. Make sure there is no blank spot keyway, (used as a guide) insert the shaft into the transmission and insure that the splines are engauged in the gear. Take a device of your choosing (or go obtain the tool) which will fit into the lip at an angle which will allow proper direction to apply force in the direction of the transmission. They usually require a tap or two to get them to seat and a little of tranny lube will help. Be careful not to damage the boot or your axel seal.||||reg||1
Z||000004||daved||05-13-2001||10:39 PM||||Thanks 777 & Resto_Guy.....finally got it!<BR>I had to resort to removing the snap ring and using my rototool with a tiny grinder to taper the leading edge.  Finished it by polishing with wet-or-dry.  Lots of lube, and two people; one to PUSHHHH...and one to guide, and "bingo", it snapped right in.<BR>Thanks guys.||||reg||1
